e ones introduced by Garsia and Remmel. Among our main results is a new proof of Guay-Paquet's elegant identity expressing the $q$-CSFs in a CSF basis with $q$-hit coefficients. We further show its equivalence to the Abreu-Nigro identity expanding the $q$-CSF in the elementary symmetric functions. This is the FPSAC extended abstract version. The full version is at ArXiv: 2104.07599.\n\n  * Counting $k$-Naples parking functions through permutations and the $k$-Naples area statistic\n\nLaura Colmenarejo, Pamela E. Harris, Zakiya Jones, Christo Keller, Andrés Ramos Rodríguez, Eunice Sukarto, Andrés R. Vindas-Meléndez\n\nSep 03 2020 math.CO arXiv:2009.01124v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2009.01124, author = {Laura Colmenarejo and Pamela E.~Harris and Zakiya Jones and Christo Keller and Andrés Ramos Rodríguez and Eunice Sukarto and Andrés R.~Vindas-Meléndez}, title = {{C}ounting $k$-{N}aples parking functions through permutations and the $k$-{N}aples area statistic}, year = {2020}, eprint = {2009.01124}, note = {arXiv:2009.01124v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe One Corner in Olde Naples the New Hotel Couldn't Fill (https://ryanchiodo.com/blog/the-one-corner-in-olde-naples-the-new-hotel-couldnt-fill)\nciteturn13search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Image: The One Corner in Olde Naples the New Hotel Couldn't Fill ... If you are thinking about your own place in this stretch of Naples, whether that means the value of a place you already own or what a move into the historic core would actually cost right now, Chiodo Group tracks the block by block detail behind stories like this one.\n\nImage: The One Corner in Olde Naples the New Hotel Couldn't Fill\n\n# The One Corner in Olde Naples the New Hotel Couldn't Fill\n\n\"You've got to be kidding me.\"\n\nThat is what restaurateur Rick Doody told his landlord on a Friday afternoon in May, the same day Naples' newest hotel welcomed its first guests two steps from his front door. Four years of permits, redesigns, and one state mediation later, Doody thought he had finally cleared the way to put tables on the sidewalk at the corner of Third Street South and Broad Avenue. Instead he got a phone call, then a termination letter, then silence.\n\nIf you live in Olde Naples, you already know the building. It sits at the edge of everything, the new hotel on one side, the shops of Third Street on the other, and it has looked exactly the same for as long as most residents can remember: handsome, restored, and completely empty.\n\n## A Deli, Then Nothing\n\nThe building at 1148 Third St. S. went up in 1921 and spent its early decades as something close to the entire civic apparatus of a young town. On paper, it read like the path to a resolution.\n\nInstead, on February 5, 2025, City Council rejected that compromise outright, and did so unanimously. Doody found out from Camalier by phone and initially misread the outcome as approval, since a unanimous vote sounds like good news until you learn which way it went.\n\n> \"It's unfortunate because it's a great corner, a great building and great potential,\" Doody said after the vote.\n\nThe fight did not end there. By Doody's account, the outdoor dining he had been chasing since 2022 finally got approved later that same year, and the approval came with a one-year grace period attached to his lease. After years of hearings, a magistrate's report, and a public reversal, the last obstacle standing between the Olde Naples Building and an open restaurant had cleared.\n\n## What Actually Changed in May\n\nThen the hotel opened, and something about the corner's calculus shifted anyway.\n\nJust five months into that one-year grace period, on the same day the Olde Naples Hotel welcomed its first guests, wrapping around two sides of the very building Doody had leased, Camalier called to say the family was reconsidering the restaurant plan. --------------